1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:06,700
Welcome to Module Seventeen, and to a new unit: niches, and the tools that let an agent fill one.

2
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:10,825
Show an ornithologist a beak, and they'll tell you what the bird eats.

3
00:00:10,825 --> 00:00:12,950
A stout cone cracks seeds.

4
00:00:12,950 --> 00:00:15,387
A long curved bill probes flowers.

5
00:00:15,387 --> 00:00:17,300
A hooked tip tears flesh.

6
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:22,350
Form fits function, and that fit is what makes an organism good at its niche.

7
00:00:22,350 --> 00:00:24,912
An agent's appendages are its tools.

8
00:00:24,912 --> 00:00:28,287
On its own, a model can only produce text.

9
00:00:28,287 --> 00:00:33,862
Tools let it look things up, calculate exactly, read your files and change things in the world.

10
00:00:33,862 --> 00:00:35,437
Why does a model need them?

11
00:00:35,437 --> 00:00:37,925
The CMU course gives two reasons.

12
00:00:37,925 --> 00:00:40,475
Some things a model simply cannot do.

13
00:00:40,475 --> 00:00:48,487
Its knowledge was frozen when training ended, so it can't know today's date, or what's in your database, without asking something.

14
00:00:48,487 --> 00:00:51,662
Other things it can do, but badly or slowly.

15
00:00:51,662 --> 00:00:58,162
It can multiply two big numbers by reasoning step by step, but a calculator is faster and never wrong.

16
00:00:58,162 --> 00:01:00,337
Here's the key idea for today.

17
00:01:00,337 --> 00:01:04,437
Unlike a beak, a tool is made of words and rules.

18
00:01:04,437 --> 00:01:07,775
It has a name, a description, and a schema.

19
00:01:07,775 --> 00:01:11,250
The schema says exactly what a valid call looks like.

20
00:01:11,250 --> 00:01:13,112
Which fields are required.

21
00:01:13,112 --> 00:01:14,850
What type each one is.

22
00:01:14,850 --> 00:01:16,650
Which values are allowed.

23
00:01:16,650 --> 00:01:18,787
Metres and hectares, yes.

24
00:01:18,787 --> 00:01:20,937
Acres, not in this tool.

25
00:01:20,937 --> 00:01:22,575
And now the clever part.

26
00:01:22,575 --> 00:01:28,050
A schema can be turned into a grammar, and the grammar can police the model while it writes.

27
00:01:28,050 --> 00:01:34,762
At each step, before a token is drawn, every token that would break the rules gets its probability set to zero.

28
00:01:34,762 --> 00:01:38,350
You've seen this move before, in the module on temperature.

29
00:01:38,350 --> 00:01:41,650
It's the same sampling step, with a filter in front of it.

30
00:01:41,650 --> 00:01:47,775
This is called constrained decoding, and it means the model's tool calls almost always come out well formed.

31
00:01:47,775 --> 00:01:49,687
But notice the word formed.

32
00:01:49,687 --> 00:01:53,300
Constrained decoding guarantees grammar, not meaning.

33
00:01:53,300 --> 00:01:58,162
A perfectly valid call can still ask for the wrong species, or the wrong file.

34
00:01:58,162 --> 00:02:00,437
So the rest of the job is design.

35
00:02:00,437 --> 00:02:07,300
Keep tools few and clearly different, because two tools with similar names cause more mistakes than one missing tool.

36
00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:12,050
Make errors that teach, saying which field was wrong and what is allowed.

37
00:02:12,050 --> 00:02:14,412
And limit what each tool can reach.

38
00:02:14,412 --> 00:02:16,825
Here's where the beak metaphor breaks.

39
00:02:16,825 --> 00:02:22,600
A beak is fixed for life, and its fit was tested by selection over generations.

40
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:26,587
Tools can be added in seconds, and nothing tests them except you.

41
00:02:26,587 --> 00:02:31,012
A finch never picks the wrong beak because the label was ambiguous.

42
00:02:31,012 --> 00:02:34,237
A model chooses tools by reading their descriptions.

43
00:02:34,237 --> 00:02:40,787
And every tool you add costs space in the context window, on every turn, whether it's used or not.

44
00:02:40,787 --> 00:02:50,137
In the lab, you'll build three tools for your capstone: a taxonomy lookup, a file reader that can only see one folder, and a unit converter.

45
00:02:50,137 --> 00:02:56,100
Then you'll give a model ten requests, some tricky, one dangerous, and watch which tool it reaches for.

46
00:02:56,100 --> 00:02:58,362
Let's go and grow some appendages.

